      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Founded in 1839 after the forced removal of the Caddo people, Greenwood grew from a frontier settlement into a thriving town.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greenwood%2C_Louisiana",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1974, citizens formed the Greenwood Pioneer Club to celebrate and preserve the town’s rich history.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://greenwoodla.org/history/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The historic Dunn House, once a Confederate hospital, was saved in 1995 by the Pioneer Club and the Women’s Club.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://greenwoodla.org/history/",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Greenwood’s 1850s economy boasted a tan yard, saddlery, distillery, brick yards, and nine saloons, drawing about 100 wagons daily.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://greenwoodla.org/history/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Greenwood’s McClurg House, built circa 1914, sits in a park named after William Peters McClurg, whose grandmother is featured in the house’s photo.",
